#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078233 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cf94b5cf-6407-5931-92df-9f87818e8a71" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Second Fundamental Theorem of Calculus"}] :question/id #uuid "25a78c7c-e5c0-5048-b12c-6ed4848f5f01" :question/math-academy-id "e-438" :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle\\dfrac {\\textrm d} {\\textrm dx} \\int_2^x \\dfrac {t^3} {t-1}\\,\\textrm d t$ for $x>2.$" :question/worked-solution "The function $\\dfrac {t^3} {t-1}$ is continuous on $[2,x],$ where $x>2.$ So FTC II applies, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}{\\int }_{2}^{x}\\frac{{t}^{3}}{t-1}\\,\\text{d}t=\\frac{{x}^{3}}{x-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078234 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f5075a64-0f91-58cf-b01b-5b71035f0680" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Second Fundamental Theorem of Calculus by Interchanging the Limits of Integration"}] :question/id #uuid "b5e8576b-cd5c-5153-a492-1e8367456744" :question/math-academy-id "e-439" :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle\\dfrac {\\textrm d} {\\textrm dx} \\int_x^0  \\dfrac{t^3}{t^2 +1}\\,\\textrm d t.$" :question/worked-solution "In this example, the variable limit $x$ is located on the lower limit. To apply FTC II, we need the variable to be on the upper limit. Using the properties of definite integrals, we can write\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int_x^0 \\dfrac{t^3}{t^2 +1} \\,\\textrm d t  = -  \\int_0^x \\dfrac{t^3}{t^2 +1}\\,\\textrm d t.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by FTC II, we have\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n \\displaystyle\\dfrac {\\textrm d} {\\textrm dx} \\int_x^0  \\dfrac{t^3}{t^2 +1} \\,\\textrm d t \n\t& \\, \\, = \\, \\, \\dfrac {\\textrm d} {\\textrm dx} \\left(-\\int_0^x \\dfrac{t^3}{t^2 +1} \\,\\textrm d t \\right)\\\n\t& \\, \\, = \\, \\, -\\dfrac {\\textrm d} {\\textrm dx} \\left(\\int_0^x \\dfrac{t^3}{t^2 +1} \\,\\textrm d t \\right)\\\n\t&= \\, \\, -\\dfrac{x^3}{x^2 +1}.}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078235 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d0703e1b-2b81-5b95-b2ad-a7e42e702c21" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Second Fundamental Theorem of Calculus Using the Chain Rule"}] :question/id #uuid "03bb67e2-fc45-591c-beaf-890929fa0e11" :question/math-academy-id "e-440" :question/problem "Find $f'(x)$ for $f(x) = \\displaystyle \\int_2^{x^2}\\arctan t\\,\\textrm d t.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute $f'(x),$  we first make the substitution $u=x^2$  and then we apply the chain rule, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}{\\int }_{2}^{{x}^{2}}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{du}}{\\text{d}x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}{\\int }_{2}^{u}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe integrand is continuous for all $t,$ so by FTC II we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\cdot \\operatorname{arctan}⁡(u) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2})\\cdot \\operatorname{arctan}⁡(u) \\\\  & =2x\\operatorname{arctan}⁡({x}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078236 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a0f285ec-4f37-5d0d-a65c-be3ca48c740f" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Second Fundamental Theorem of Calculus With Two Variable Limits"}] :question/id #uuid "bda18914-8a35-5d61-9b6d-d2747035e80b" :question/math-academy-id "e-441" :question/problem "Find $f'(1)$ if  $\\displaystyle f(x) = \\int_x^{x^2} (t^2 + 2t)  \\,\\, \\textrm d t.$" :question/worked-solution "Here, both the lower and upper limits contain a variable. However, to apply FTC II, we need the variable to be on the upper limit only. So, we can split the integral into two integrals, and rewrite them so that in both cases the variable is on the upper limit. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{x}^{{x}^{2}}({t}^{2}+2t)\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{x}^{a}({t}^{2}+2t)\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{a}^{{x}^{2}}({t}^{2}+2t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{a}^{x}({t}^{2}+2t)\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{a}^{{x}^{2}}({t}^{2}+2t)\\,\\text{d}t,\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is any constant number between $x$ and $x^2.$\n\nThe integrand $t^2 + 2t$ is continuous for all $t,$ so applying FTC II gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-{\\int }_{a}^{x}({t}^{2}+2t)\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{a}^{{x}^{2}}({t}^{2}+2t)\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({\\int }_{a}^{x}({t}^{2}+2t)\\,\\text{d}t)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({\\int }_{a}^{{x}^{2}}({t}^{2}+2t)\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =-({x}^{2}+2x)+\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}({\\int }_{a}^{u}({t}^{2}+2t)\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =-{x}^{2}-2x+2x({u}^{2}+2u) \\\\  & =-{x}^{2}-2x+2x(({x}^{2})^{2}+2{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =-{x}^{2}-2x+2x({x}^{4}+2{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =-{x}^{2}-2x+2{x}^{5}+4{x}^{3} \\\\  & =2{x}^{5}+4{x}^{3}-{x}^{2}-2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we used the substitution $u=x^2,$ as before.\n\nFinally, we can compute $f'(1).$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(1) & =2(1)^{5}+4(1)^{3}-(1)^{2}-2(1) \\\\  & =2+4-1-2 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319285 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cf94b5cf-6407-5931-92df-9f87818e8a71" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Second Fundamental Theorem of Calculus"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "71c1f60e-96ea-55f2-a5f2-b7e0973c6272" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{e}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319287} {:answer/id #uuid "adf9fa8b-0215-5d18-98fd-abc4fc8d1a49" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319288} {:answer/id #uuid "06090104-7633-5e00-9938-e3bbd7c27ece" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319289} {:answer/id #uuid "2368dcd8-f3de-5a02-8f0f-c92165ec8e8e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319290} {:answer/id #uuid "0c649abc-e469-5ae7-92e0-4558cc6103cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{e}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319291}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0c649abc-e469-5ae7-92e0-4558cc6103cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{e}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319291} :answer-field/id #uuid "96e0731b-1d21-5e11-83d4-edbd8703284c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319286}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8f0f42de-b5f1-5a07-8a0d-c5f6caca1908" :question/math-academy-id "q-116475" :question/problem "If $g(x)={\\int }_{0}^{x}{t}^{2}{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t$ for $x>0,$ determine ${g}^{′}(2).$" :question/worked-solution "The function ${t}^{2}{e}^{t}$ is continuous on $[0,x],$ where $x>0.$ So, FTC II applies, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}{\\int }_{0}^{x}{t}^{2}{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={x}^{2}{e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the value of ${g}^{′}(2),$ we substitute $x=2$ in the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(2) & ={2}^{2}\\cdot {e}^{2} \\\\  & =4{e}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294483 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a0f285ec-4f37-5d0d-a65c-be3ca48c740f" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Second Fundamental Theorem of Calculus With Two Variable Limits"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08dad672-a923-542a-b2a4-8075d5a00c84" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\operatorname{cos}⁡1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294485} {:answer/id #uuid "b87a0cd5-ed42-5831-8b73-6ef14d1fe940" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294486} {:answer/id #uuid "622002d4-df72-5060-b2ac-6b231db87adc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294487} {:answer/id #uuid "e352e279-23cc-5fd6-a6f9-5c667b866ab0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2-\\operatorname{cos}⁡1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294488} {:answer/id #uuid "87f994a6-39cb-592b-ad83-8b7228910a6e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294489}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "08dad672-a923-542a-b2a4-8075d5a00c84" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\operatorname{cos}⁡1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294485} :answer-field/id #uuid "eb530fa1-c008-55fc-925b-31d688efd41d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294484}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e902d8d9-f9a5-5a6b-9ee4-5e36cb9511d0" :question/math-academy-id "q-117278" :question/problem "Find ${f}^{′}(1)$ if $f(x)={\\int }_{-{x}^{3}}^{x}{t}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.$" :question/worked-solution "Here, both the lower and upper limits contain a variable. However, to apply FTC II, we need the variable to be on the upper limit only. So, we can split the integral into two integrals, and rewrite them so that in both cases the variable is on the upper limit. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-{x}^{3}}^{x}{t}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{-{x}^{3}}^{a}{t}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{a}^{x}{t}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{a}^{-{x}^{3}}{t}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{a}^{x}{t}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is any constant number between $-{x}^{3}$ and $x.$\n\nThe integrand ${t}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$ is continuous for all $t,$ so applying the FTC II gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-{\\int }_{a}^{-{x}^{3}}{t}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{a}^{x}{t}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({\\int }_{a}^{-{x}^{3}}{t}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({\\int }_{a}^{x}{t}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}({\\int }_{a}^{u}{t}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t)+{x}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\  & =-(-3{x}^{2}){u}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡u+{x}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\  & =3{x}^{2}{(-{x}^{3})}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-{x}^{3})+{x}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\  & =3{x}^{14}\\operatorname{cos}⁡{x}^{3}+{x}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we used the substitution $u=-{x}^{3}.$\n\nFinally, we can compute ${f}^{′}(1).$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(1) & =3(1)^{14}\\operatorname{cos}⁡{1}^{3}+{1}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡1 \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡1 \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319292 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a0f285ec-4f37-5d0d-a65c-be3ca48c740f" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Second Fundamental Theorem of Calculus With Two Variable Limits"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "94e7b54e-df1b-564b-b397-7d7a260ed3d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319294} {:answer/id #uuid "c9e568fc-550a-5922-ace2-4495328f1423" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319295} {:answer/id #uuid "04bfafa5-fff9-51cb-b195-6f5f762d519a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319296} {:answer/id #uuid "99a92494-e0cf-52de-ab02-c000b4dbf8c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319297} {:answer/id #uuid "7e7753f8-282c-5360-ba7a-676831fb28da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319298}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "94e7b54e-df1b-564b-b397-7d7a260ed3d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319294} :answer-field/id #uuid "05575e57-4021-5a57-b22c-aa88d47b637a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319293}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "1a5fda93-db37-5201-9a1b-f45b1acfcd5a" :question/math-academy-id "q-117289" :question/problem "Find ${f}^{′}(1)$ if $f(x)={\\int }_{x}^{\\sqrt{x}}\\frac{1}{{t}^{4}+3}\\,\\text{d}t.$" :question/worked-solution "Here, both the lower and upper limits contain a variable. However, to apply FTC II, we need the variable to be on the upper limit only. So, we can split the integral into two integrals, and rewrite them so that in both cases the variable is on the upper limit. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{x}^{\\sqrt{x}}\\frac{1}{{t}^{4}+3}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{x}^{a}\\frac{1}{{t}^{4}+3}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{a}^{\\sqrt{x}}\\frac{1}{{t}^{4}+3}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{a}^{x}\\frac{1}{{t}^{4}+3}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{a}^{\\sqrt{x}}\\frac{1}{{t}^{4}+3}\\,\\text{d}t,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is any constant number between $x$ and $\\sqrt{x}.$\n\nThe integrand $\\frac{1}{{t}^{4}+3}$ is continuous for all $t,$ so applying the FTC II gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-{\\int }_{a}^{x}\\frac{1}{{t}^{4}+3}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{a}^{\\sqrt{x}}\\frac{1}{{t}^{4}+3}\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({\\int }_{a}^{x}\\frac{1}{{t}^{4}+3}\\,\\text{d}t)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({\\int }_{a}^{\\sqrt{x}}\\frac{1}{{t}^{4}+3}\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}+3}+\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}({\\int }_{a}^{u}\\frac{1}{{t}^{4}+3}\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}+3}+\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{u}^{4}+3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}+3}+\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}((\\sqrt{x})^{4}+3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}({x}^{2}+3)}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}+3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we used the substitution $u=\\sqrt{x}.$\n\nFinally, we can compute ${f}^{′}(1).$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(1) & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{1}({1}^{2}+3)}-\\frac{1}{{1}^{4}+3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}-\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319299 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cf94b5cf-6407-5931-92df-9f87818e8a71" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Second Fundamental Theorem of Calculus"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b50014eb-37b1-5e7e-b39e-79967c5f2b03" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+2x+5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319301} {:answer/id #uuid "91a298e4-ddea-531b-a878-eb9e67f73cac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319302} {:answer/id #uuid "1f9989e1-a37a-5179-934b-0e67516f6de2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}-2x+5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319303} {:answer/id #uuid "80bd4f7a-710e-59cd-9e0f-c940a40b5018" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x+5x-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319304} {:answer/id #uuid "f80f8d18-7241-5132-a755-c8d2b2d5d4a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319305}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1f9989e1-a37a-5179-934b-0e67516f6de2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}-2x+5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319303} :answer-field/id #uuid "abaf9c92-8c4f-5b30-a893-c6d9a79404fd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319300}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2c58494e-2f61-5da1-b978-8b08a71602bf" :question/math-academy-id "q-30618" :question/problem "Calculate\n$\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}{\\int }_{-1}^{x}({t}^{2}-2t+5)\\,\\text{d}t$\nfor $x>-1.$" :question/worked-solution "The function ${t}^{2}-2t+5$ is continuous on $[-1,x]$ where $x>-1.$ So, FTC II applies, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}{\\int }_{-1}^{x}({t}^{2}-2t+5)\\,\\text{d}t & ={x}^{2}-2x+5.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319306 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cf94b5cf-6407-5931-92df-9f87818e8a71" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Second Fundamental Theorem of Calculus"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9e350179-f6ce-543f-bf52-0d1f02eb6646" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{x}^{3}+b{x}^{2}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319308} {:answer/id #uuid "a4d8b4a0-d0ce-5544-846e-23a9dfe403f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319309} {:answer/id #uuid "99d2a0fe-f625-54c9-962e-bc301fb38ede" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319310} {:answer/id #uuid "f69e7616-6f5b-552d-9533-ee47d2b14ff7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319311} {:answer/id #uuid "7b58277e-ae12-5d1b-becd-8434c1cf8aed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a{x}^{3}+b{x}^{2}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319312}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7b58277e-ae12-5d1b-becd-8434c1cf8aed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a{x}^{3}+b{x}^{2}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319312} :answer-field/id #uuid "1ff3e925-d30c-593d-a8ab-d1f17f3b5217" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319307}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0dea6a81-65f3-53b1-bed9-5b968fb5bb13" :question/math-academy-id "q-30627" :question/problem "Calculate\n$\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}{\\int }_{-5}^{x}(a{s}^{3}+b{s}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}s$\nfor $x>-5,$ where $a$ and $b$ are constants." :question/worked-solution "The function $a{s}^{3}+b{s}^{2}+1$ is continuous on $[-5,x],$ where $x>-5.$ So, FTC II applies, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}{\\int }_{-5}^{x}(a{s}^{3}+b{s}^{2}+1)\\text{ }\\,\\text{d}t & =a{x}^{3}+b{x}^{2}+1.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319313 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f5075a64-0f91-58cf-b01b-5b71035f0680" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Second Fundamental Theorem of Calculus by Interchanging the Limits of Integration"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "63ff3cba-ad6a-5e94-bd5e-b12e583ca126" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\sin(x^2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319315}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "63ff3cba-ad6a-5e94-bd5e-b12e583ca126" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\sin(x^2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319315} :answer-field/id #uuid "558852eb-4f01-5cbd-b343-42bd170341c9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319314}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "964d2478-77e2-59e8-841e-e018ad51c322" :question/math-academy-id "q-316373" :question/problem "If $x<2,$ then $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}{\\int }_{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({t}^{2})\\,\\text{d}t=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Here, the variable limit $x$ appears as the lower limit of integration. To apply FTC II directly, we prefer the variable on the upper limit. Using the property of definite integrals, we rewrite\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({t}^{2})\\,\\text{d}t=-{\\int }_{2}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({t}^{2})\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nNow, by FTC II we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}{\\int }_{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({t}^{2})\\,\\text{d}t & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-{\\int }_{2}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({t}^{2})\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}{\\int }_{2}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({t}^{2})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319316 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f5075a64-0f91-58cf-b01b-5b71035f0680" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Second Fundamental Theorem of Calculus by Interchanging the Limits of Integration"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "33e429fa-0574-5d0a-9d11-643300181573" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4e^6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319318} {:answer/id #uuid "fea009ec-e891-50b7-8ac1-faaacc48e800" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4e^6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319319}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "33e429fa-0574-5d0a-9d11-643300181573" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4e^6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319318} :answer-field/id #uuid "dd29bc71-9add-50cd-8034-bbbf0e144829" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319317}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "bbf23fd5-c7a9-5978-b5c2-9ee5483a0885" :question/math-academy-id "q-316389" :question/problem "Let $f(t)={t}^{2}{e}^{3t}.$ Find ${h}^{′}(2)$ given the function $h$ is defined by $h(x)={\\int }_{x}^{3}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t$ for $x<3.$\n\n${h}^{′}(2)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "In this example, the variable limit $x$ is located on the lower limit. To apply FTC II, we prefer the variable to be on the upper limit. Using the properties of definite integrals, we can write\n\n$$\nh(x)={\\int }_{x}^{3}{t}^{2}{e}^{3t}\\,\\text{d}t=-{\\int }_{3}^{x}{t}^{2}{e}^{3t}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by FTC II, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}{\\int }_{x}^{3}{t}^{2}{e}^{3t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-{\\int }_{3}^{x}{t}^{2}{e}^{3t}\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({\\int }_{3}^{x}{t}^{2}{e}^{3t}\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =-{x}^{2}{e}^{3x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the value of ${h}^{′}(2),$ we substitute $x=2$ in the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{′}(2) & =-{2}^{2}\\cdot {e}^{3(2)} \\\\  & =-4{e}^{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319320 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d0703e1b-2b81-5b95-b2ad-a7e42e702c21" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Second Fundamental Theorem of Calculus Using the Chain Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b2f4d357-a6b9-5c57-9ef9-4a0ecc205abb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^x\\cos(e^{3x})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319322} {:answer/id #uuid "2564531a-7553-506e-9554-e8994460b02a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-e^x\\cos(e^{3x})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319323}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2564531a-7553-506e-9554-e8994460b02a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-e^x\\cos(e^{3x})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319323} :answer-field/id #uuid "eb542cc0-a312-5033-b7ea-41072d444b9b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319321}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "64a8ab90-732d-5ddf-a15d-bdcfaa0872b0" :question/math-academy-id "q-316401" :question/problem "Find ${g}^{′}(x)$ for $g(x)={\\int }_{{e}^{x}}^{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({t}^{3})\\,\\text{d}t.$\n\n${g}^{′}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To compute ${g}^{′}(x),$ we first make the substitution $u={e}^{x}$ and then we apply the chain rule, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}{\\int }_{{e}^{x}}^{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({t}^{3})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}{\\int }_{u}^{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({t}^{3})\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe limit $u$ is located on the lower limit. To apply FTC II, we need the variable to be on the upper limit. Using the properties of definite integrals, we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}{\\int }_{u}^{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({t}^{3})\\,\\text{d}t & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}(-{\\int }_{5}^{u}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({t}^{3})\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}{\\int }_{5}^{u}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({t}^{3})\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by FTC II, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}{\\int }_{u}^{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({t}^{3})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\cdot (-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}{\\int }_{5}^{u}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({t}^{3})\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\cdot (-\\operatorname{cos}⁡({u}^{3})) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{x})\\cdot (-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(({e}^{x})^{3})) \\\\  & =-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({e}^{3x}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319324 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f5075a64-0f91-58cf-b01b-5b71035f0680" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Second Fundamental Theorem of Calculus by Interchanging the Limits of Integration"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9117a9b6-8ab8-53fe-866f-6acd9182c966" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319326} {:answer/id #uuid "601f0262-e552-5bea-9eec-760a76177e8f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319327}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "601f0262-e552-5bea-9eec-760a76177e8f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319327} :answer-field/id #uuid "410a2e5f-ab0d-5735-9de9-e05503b26a33" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319325}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7c89f865-0b83-58a4-aac0-5e0e25249852" :question/math-academy-id "q-332067" :question/problem "Let $f(t)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.$ Find ${h}^{′}(\\pi )$ given the function $h$ is defined by $h(x)={\\int }_{x}^{2\\pi }f(t)\\,\\text{d}t$ for $0<x<2\\pi .$\n\n${h}^{′}(\\pi )=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "In this example, the variable limit $x$ is located on the lower limit. To apply FTC II, we prefer the variable to be on the upper limit. Using the properties of definite integrals, we can write\n\n$$\nh(x)={\\int }_{x}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{ln}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t=-{\\int }_{2\\pi }^{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by FTC II, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}{\\int }_{x}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{ln}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-{\\int }_{2\\pi }^{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({\\int }_{2\\pi }^{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the value of ${h}^{′}(\\pi ),$ we substitute $x=\\pi $ in the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{′}(\\pi ) & =-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\pi )\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi ) \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\pi \\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319328 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f5075a64-0f91-58cf-b01b-5b71035f0680" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Second Fundamental Theorem of Calculus by Interchanging the Limits of Integration"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "24d92f18-9896-5815-a91e-2ac8de777387" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319330}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "24d92f18-9896-5815-a91e-2ac8de777387" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319330} :answer-field/id #uuid "19dba24c-54b5-516b-8240-fe1fff592bd1" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319329}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "bd3e742b-7240-59fc-94f4-6663ac30d7db" :question/math-academy-id "q-332071" :question/problem "Let $f(t)=2{t}^{3}+{t}^{2}-1.$ Find ${h}^{′}(0)$ given the function $h$ is defined by $h(x)={\\int }_{x}^{1}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t$ for $x<1.$\n\n${h}^{′}(0)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "In this example, the variable limit $x$ is located on the lower limit. To apply FTC II, we need the variable to be on the upper limit. Using the properties of definite integrals, we can write\n\n$$\nh(x)={\\int }_{x}^{1}(2{t}^{3}+{t}^{2}-1)\\,\\text{d}t=-{\\int }_{1}^{x}(2{t}^{3}+{t}^{2}-1)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by FTC II, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}{\\int }_{x}^{1}(2{t}^{3}+{t}^{2}-1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-{\\int }_{1}^{x}(2{t}^{3}+{t}^{2}-1)\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({\\int }_{1}^{x}(2{t}^{3}+{t}^{2}-1)\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =-(2{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the value of ${h}^{′}(0),$ we substitute $x=0$ in the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{′}(0) & =-(2\\cdot {0}^{3}+{0}^{2}-1) \\\\  & =-(0+0-1) \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319331 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d0703e1b-2b81-5b95-b2ad-a7e42e702c21" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Second Fundamental Theorem of Calculus Using the Chain Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c51ff18-4f24-571b-9e30-6b40ac4b542c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3x^2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319333}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2c51ff18-4f24-571b-9e30-6b40ac4b542c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3x^2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319333} :answer-field/id #uuid "76ab5b15-4d7c-5988-9774-8dc401899170" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319332}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "110184fb-4f4b-5c53-bfd3-fe4e1173890d" :question/math-academy-id "q-333563" :question/problem "Find ${g}^{′}(x)$ if $g(x)={\\int }_{0}^{{x}^{3}+1}{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t.$\n\n${g}^{′}(x)=$ \n{{field-1}} ${e}^{{x}^{3}+1}$" :question/worked-solution "To compute ${g}^{′}(x),$ we first make the substitution $u={x}^{3}+1$ and then we apply the chain rule, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}{\\int }_{0}^{{x}^{3}+1}{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}{\\int }_{0}^{u}{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe integrand is continuous on $[0,u].$ Therefore, by FTC II, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}{\\int }_{0}^{u}{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\cdot {e}^{u} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{3}+1)\\cdot {e}^{{x}^{3}+1} \\\\  & =3{x}^{2}\\cdot {e}^{{x}^{3}+1} \\\\  & =3{x}^{2}\\,{e}^{{x}^{3}+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319334 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d0703e1b-2b81-5b95-b2ad-a7e42e702c21" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Second Fundamental Theorem of Calculus Using the Chain Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "047b6330-dc47-5782-a93c-4e203e18aa67" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3x^{11}-6x^5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319336}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "047b6330-dc47-5782-a93c-4e203e18aa67" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3x^{11}-6x^5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319336} :answer-field/id #uuid "5fcd6320-af3b-50cb-8b93-61aee974c5ea" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319335}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "caf3e0e6-df65-5a67-9c9b-e28b29e53d7d" :question/math-academy-id "q-333573" :question/problem "Find ${g}^{′}(x)$ if $g(x)={\\int }_{0}^{{x}^{3}}({t}^{3}-2t)\\,\\text{d}t.$\n\n${g}^{′}(x)=$ \n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To compute ${g}^{′}(x),$ we first make the substitution $u={x}^{3}$ and then we apply the chain rule, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}{\\int }_{0}^{{x}^{3}}({t}^{3}-2t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}{\\int }_{0}^{u}({t}^{3}-2t)\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe integrand is continuous on $[0,u].$ Therefore, by FTC II, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}{\\int }_{0}^{u}({t}^{3}-2t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\cdot ({u}^{3}-2u) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{3})\\cdot (({x}^{3})^{3}-2({x}^{3})) \\\\  & =3{x}^{2}\\cdot ({x}^{9}-2{x}^{3}) \\\\  & =3{x}^{2}({x}^{9}-2{x}^{3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319337 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a0f285ec-4f37-5d0d-a65c-be3ca48c740f" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Second Fundamental Theorem of Calculus With Two Variable Limits"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e4efc0b0-5690-5dcb-bda3-90185ceaff37" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319339}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e4efc0b0-5690-5dcb-bda3-90185ceaff37" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319339} :answer-field/id #uuid "b9daacc8-19e7-529a-9a8a-0c2f8745cf0d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319338}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "36b5cbf2-4547-50e0-a29e-914ff91501a4" :question/math-academy-id "q-333882" :question/problem "If $G(x)={\\int }_{x}^{\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x)}(1+{t}^{2})^{2}\\,\\text{d}t,$ then ${G}^{′}(0)=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Here, both the lower and upper limits contain a variable. However, to apply FTC II, we need the variable to be on the upper limit only. So, we can split the integral into two integrals and rewrite them so that in both cases the variable is on the upper limit. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{x}^{\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x)}(1+{t}^{2})^{2}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{x}^{a}(1+{t}^{2})^{2}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{a}^{\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x)}(1+{t}^{2})^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{a}^{x}(1+{t}^{2})^{2}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{a}^{\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x)}(1+{t}^{2})^{2}\\,\\text{d}t,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is any constant number between $x$ and $\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x).$\n\nThe integrand $(1+{t}^{2})^{2}$ is continuous for all $t.$ Therefore, applying the FTC II, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{G}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-{\\int }_{a}^{x}(1+{t}^{2})^{2}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{a}^{\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x)}(1+{t}^{2})^{2}\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({\\int }_{a}^{x}(1+{t}^{2})^{2}\\,\\text{d}t)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({\\int }_{a}^{\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x)}(1+{t}^{2})^{2}\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({\\int }_{a}^{x}(1+{t}^{2})^{2}\\,\\text{d}t)+\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}({\\int }_{a}^{\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x)}(1+{t}^{2})^{2}\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =-(1+{x}^{2})^{2}+\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\cdot {(1+{u}^{2})}^{2} \\\\  & =-(1+{x}^{2})^{2}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x))\\cdot {(1+{tan}^{2}⁡(x))}^{2} \\\\  & =-(1+{x}^{2})^{2}+{sec}^{2}⁡(x)\\cdot {(1+{tan}^{2}⁡(x))}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we used the substitution $u=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x).$\n\nFinally, we can compute ${G}^{′}(0).$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{G}^{′}(0) & =-(1+{0}^{2})^{2}+{sec}^{2}⁡(0)\\cdot {(1+{tan}^{2}⁡(0))}^{2} \\\\  & =-1+1\\cdot {1}^{2} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319340 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a0f285ec-4f37-5d0d-a65c-be3ca48c740f" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Second Fundamental Theorem of Calculus With Two Variable Limits"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08d345e1-830a-5cef-b585-ba6bb99c16b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319342} {:answer/id #uuid "644d5b2b-40e5-501b-9822-e49577403178" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319343}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "644d5b2b-40e5-501b-9822-e49577403178" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319343} :answer-field/id #uuid "9ca5ae5b-cd4d-533c-b91b-1a241d120141" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319341}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "845f0089-00c1-55c9-9061-c3bdb326a9aa" :question/math-academy-id "q-333952" :question/problem "If $G(x)={\\int }_{x}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)}{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t,$ then ${G}^{′}(0)=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Here, both the lower and upper limits contain a variable. However, to apply FTC II, we need the variable to be on the upper limit only. So, we can split the integral into two integrals and rewrite them so that in both cases the variable is on the upper limit. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{x}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)}{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{x}^{a}{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{a}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)}{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{a}^{x}{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{a}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)}{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is any constant number between $x$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x).$\n\nThe integrand ${e}^{t}$ is continuous for all $t.$ Therefore, applying the FTC II, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{G}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-{\\int }_{a}^{x}{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{a}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)}{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({\\int }_{a}^{x}{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({\\int }_{a}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)}{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({\\int }_{a}^{x}{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t)+\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}({\\int }_{a}^{u}{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =-{e}^{x}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x))\\cdot {e}^{u} \\\\  & =-{e}^{x}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x))\\cdot {e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)} \\\\  & =-{e}^{x}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\cdot {e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we used the substitution $u=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x).$\n\nFinally, we can compute ${G}^{′}(0).$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{G}^{′}(0) & =-{e}^{0}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)\\cdot {e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)} \\\\  & =-1-0 \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078858 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cf94b5cf-6407-5931-92df-9f87818e8a71" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Second Fundamental Theorem of Calculus"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "90e52d69-8841-5a40-ac03-0698dcc64b01" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x\\cos x}{x^{2} + 4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076360} {:answer/id #uuid "096683ff-5124-5438-bd0b-693462ab80b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sin x}{x^{2}} - \\frac{\\sin 3}{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076361} {:answer/id #uuid "375135a4-a4c7-59ab-8a5c-38cbfbf7f8cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\cos x}{x^{2} + 4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076362} {:answer/id #uuid "69b43c13-0fef-5753-90c6-67be9bed5aea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\cos x}{x^{2} + 4} - \\frac{\\cos 3}{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076363} {:answer/id #uuid "7db5dcfc-d1c0-505a-9de0-9189c2ed20bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\cos x}{x^{2}} + \\frac{\\cos 3}{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076364}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "375135a4-a4c7-59ab-8a5c-38cbfbf7f8cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\cos x}{x^{2} + 4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076362} :answer-field/id #uuid "e033e820-ed37-5fc2-988c-a05e29446d29" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076359}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0138eec2-eff9-517a-845d-ed8e6a92a2fa" :question/math-academy-id "q-4552" :question/problem "Calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}{\\int }_{3}^{x}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{{t}^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}t$ for $x>3.$" :question/worked-solution "The function $\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{{t}^{2}+4}$ is continuous on $[3,x],$ where $x>3.$ So, FTC II applies, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}{\\int }_{3}^{x}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{{t}^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}t & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{{x}^{2}+4}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319344 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d0703e1b-2b81-5b95-b2ad-a7e42e702c21" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Second Fundamental Theorem of Calculus Using the Chain Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5e7b05ec-b43b-5adb-88e5-a3ddf5b979de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319346} {:answer/id #uuid "ef5aa62e-68ee-5a37-a279-604349a87ca4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319347} {:answer/id #uuid "5f876eac-832a-5e8a-a18c-ab96aa548409" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319348} {:answer/id #uuid "696cdcc2-51da-5b11-860d-d7e89bf3c2d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319349} {:answer/id #uuid "5b00ba3a-496f-58be-9948-56ee8204f8a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319350}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5f876eac-832a-5e8a-a18c-ab96aa548409" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319348} :answer-field/id #uuid "6013b560-f643-52c0-997d-0659c95349b6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319345}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c07e1de1-cd19-51e2-9521-40cd741e9f90" :question/math-academy-id "q-4553" :question/problem "Find ${f}^{′}(2)$ if $f(x)={\\int }_{2}^{2x}\\sqrt{{t}^{3}-t}\\,\\text{d}t.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute ${f}^{′}(x),$ we first make the substitution $u=2x$ and then we apply the chain rule, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}{\\int }_{2}^{2x}\\sqrt{{t}^{3}-t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}{\\int }_{2}^{u}\\sqrt{{t}^{3}-t}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe integrand is continuous on $[2,u],$ and so by FTC II we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\cdot \\sqrt{{u}^{3}-u} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2x)\\cdot \\sqrt{(2x)^{3}-(2x)} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{8{x}^{3}-2x}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the value of ${f}^{′}(2),$ we substitute $x=2$ in the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(2) & =2\\sqrt{8(2)^{3}-2(2)} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{64-4} \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{15}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319351 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d0703e1b-2b81-5b95-b2ad-a7e42e702c21" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Second Fundamental Theorem of Calculus Using the Chain Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2750f188-1555-5a7c-bf96-b1a2e625394f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319353} {:answer/id #uuid "b362d609-9c9f-569c-a798-c880a9381f08" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319354} {:answer/id #uuid "19c69a97-5494-57e3-a028-bdbadf73474d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319355} {:answer/id #uuid "c86282f8-f50b-5e80-afbb-504815f405f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319356} {:answer/id #uuid "fd45bf03-6e24-54f0-9207-b2f04081d85a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319357}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b362d609-9c9f-569c-a798-c880a9381f08" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319354} :answer-field/id #uuid "9f7b4c45-d028-59ab-8a8f-0f782cb30f5a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319352}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "253bec95-1329-5337-b512-00619d2a4dbc" :question/math-academy-id "q-4776" :question/problem "For $x>0,$ find ${f}^{′}(x)$ if $f(x)={\\int }_{-{x}^{2}}^{-4}\\frac{1}{t}\\,\\text{d}t.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute ${f}^{′}(x),$ we first make the substitution $u=-{x}^{2}$ and then we apply the chain rule, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}{\\int }_{-{x}^{2}}^{-4}\\frac{1}{t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}{\\int }_{u}^{-4}\\frac{1}{t}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe limit $u$ is located on the lower limit. To apply FTC II, we need the variable to be on the upper limit. Using the properties of the definite integrals, we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}{\\int }_{u}^{-4}\\frac{1}{t}\\,\\text{d}t & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}(-{\\int }_{-4}^{u}\\frac{1}{t}\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}{\\int }_{-4}^{u}\\frac{1}{t}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by FTC II, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\cdot (-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}{\\int }_{-4}^{u}\\frac{1}{t}\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{u}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-{x}^{2})\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{u}) \\\\  & =-2x\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{-{x}^{2}}) \\\\  & =-2x\\cdot \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{x}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319358 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f5075a64-0f91-58cf-b01b-5b71035f0680" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Second Fundamental Theorem of Calculus by Interchanging the Limits of Integration"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a02138fc-c356-5f9d-9305-9ecc85184c34" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319360} {:answer/id #uuid "76f59966-3352-5b9e-97e4-746ee41c03b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-(x+4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319361} {:answer/id #uuid "14d0f2ad-92d5-5fc2-a2a2-6848e63e9d1d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2x+7}{x-2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319362} {:answer/id #uuid "5e02efc0-75af-5b6a-bb13-a33fca702adb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{{x}^{2}+7x+12}{x-2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319363} {:answer/id #uuid "9aacb5e1-201d-5d08-9edc-a0837653558d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-(x+5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319364}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5e02efc0-75af-5b6a-bb13-a33fca702adb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{{x}^{2}+7x+12}{x-2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319363} :answer-field/id #uuid "761fa7ca-df8b-51ac-b133-cc164da689b5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319359}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "94fa79a1-d796-5df1-8f75-14242dc59a6d" :question/math-academy-id "q-75217" :question/problem "Calculate\n$\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}{\\int }_{x}^{0}\\frac{{t}^{2}+7t+12}{t-2}\\,\\text{d}t$\nfor $x<0.$" :question/worked-solution "In this example, the variable limit $x$ is located on the lower limit. To apply FTC II, we need the variable to be on the upper limit. Using the properties of definite integrals, we can write\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{x}^{0}\\frac{{t}^{2}+7t+12}{t-2}\\,\\text{d}t=-{\\int }_{0}^{x}\\frac{{t}^{2}+7t+12}{t-2}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by FTC II, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}{\\int }_{x}^{0}\\frac{{t}^{2}+7t+12}{t-2}\\,\\text{d}t & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-{\\int }_{0}^{x}\\frac{{t}^{2}+7t+12}{t-2}\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({\\int }_{0}^{x}\\frac{{t}^{2}+7t+12}{t-2}\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =-\\frac{{x}^{2}+7x+12}{x-2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319365 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a0f285ec-4f37-5d0d-a65c-be3ca48c740f" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Second Fundamental Theorem of Calculus With Two Variable Limits"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "366d8039-7f11-582a-8502-6deb1a331a86" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{e}{2+{e}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{{e}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319367} {:answer/id #uuid "5c468f91-19d1-520d-b09a-94666178fd8c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{e}{2+{e}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{3e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319368} {:answer/id #uuid "b52753b0-1bf4-5820-8b27-afea450c25ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319369} {:answer/id #uuid "53ca0fd6-7484-511e-b736-5a2e1a1d8021" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{e}{2+{e}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319370} {:answer/id #uuid "a13a3755-7b59-5e30-a433-f21ece8342ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{e}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319371}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5c468f91-19d1-520d-b09a-94666178fd8c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{e}{2+{e}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{3e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319368} :answer-field/id #uuid "a59c82ba-af0b-5b0e-8ff5-2ec31d060609" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319366}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "b368aef2-d174-5fff-ab87-437a429b2144" :question/math-academy-id "q-75458" :question/problem "Find ${F}^{′}(e)$ if $F(x)={\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)}^{x}\\frac{t}{2+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.$" :question/worked-solution "Here, both the lower and upper limits contain a variable. However, to apply FTC II, we need the variable to be on the upper limit only. So, we can split the integral into two integrals, and rewrite them so that in both cases the variable is on the upper limit. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)}^{x}\\frac{t}{2+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)}^{a}\\frac{t}{2+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{a}^{x}\\frac{t}{2+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{a}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)}\\frac{t}{2+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{a}^{x}\\frac{t}{2+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is any constant number between $\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ and $x.$\n\nThe integrand $\\frac{t}{2+{t}^{2}}$ is continuous for all $t,$ so applying FTC II gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-{\\int }_{a}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)}\\frac{t}{2+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{a}^{x}\\frac{t}{2+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({\\int }_{a}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)}\\frac{t}{2+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({\\int }_{a}^{x}\\frac{t}{2+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\text{du}}{\\text{d}x}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}({\\int }_{a}^{u}\\frac{t}{2+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t)+\\frac{x}{2+{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{x}(\\frac{u}{2+{u}^{2}})+\\frac{x}{2+{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{x}(\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)}{2+(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)^{2}})+\\frac{x}{2+{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{2+{x}^{2}}-\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)}{2x+x(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x))^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we used the substitution $u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x).$\n\nFinally, we can compute ${F}^{′}(e).$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(e) & =\\frac{e}{2+{e}^{2}}-\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡e}{2e+e(\\operatorname{ln}⁡e)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{e}{2+{e}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{2e+e} \\\\  & =\\frac{e}{2+{e}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{3e}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319372 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cf94b5cf-6407-5931-92df-9f87818e8a71" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Second Fundamental Theorem of Calculus"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6ebceae7-ba05-5111-8b16-99f49869c7bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{g}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319374} {:answer/id #uuid "74101cae-c8db-5f43-a5ea-15ecae944e59" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{g}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319375} {:answer/id #uuid "f3534a66-3b14-5162-b9d2-a59d8ca5268f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{g}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319376} {:answer/id #uuid "967b3ee4-7a82-5278-b0dd-3f2ae427c2b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{g}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319377} {:answer/id #uuid "d59fbc42-ee86-50ac-aa25-ceeaf2a462d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{g}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=\\frac{\\pi \\sqrt[3]{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319378}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "74101cae-c8db-5f43-a5ea-15ecae944e59" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{g}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319375} :answer-field/id #uuid "c8ba2c16-ce21-568a-856d-aa74fb258a7e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319373}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8b31d2f7-a6e0-5ec4-9743-3d458a957ec1" :question/math-academy-id "q-75583" :question/problem "Let $f(t)=t\\sqrt[3]{\\operatorname{tan}⁡t}.$ Find ${g}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})$ given the function $g$ is defined by $g(x)={\\int }_{0}^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t$ for $0<x<\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "The function $f(t)=t\\sqrt[3]{\\operatorname{tan}⁡t}$ is continuous on $[0,x],$ where $0<x<\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ So, FTC II applies, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}{\\int }_{0}^{x}t\\sqrt[3]{\\operatorname{tan}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =x\\sqrt[3]{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the value of ${g}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}),$ we substitute $x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ into the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\sqrt[3]{\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\sqrt[3]{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
